<!--
  Two pages, two URLs. ?spot=, ?type=, ?lang= and ?style= are deep links into
  the map and the canonical tag points them all back to it, so listing them
  would only ask Google to index the same page sixty times.

  /lists is the second one, and it is here because it is the only page that
  links the lists together. Every public list is indexable and always has been,
  but each was an island a crawler could reach only if somebody had posted the
  link somewhere. The individual lists are still not listed here — there are as
  many of them as people have made, they change as people write, and this page
  is the way in to all of them.

  It was /lists/public until today, and /lists/kept before that. Both still
  answer, as 301s, and neither is listed here: a sitemap is for the address a
  page is at, and a forwarded one asks a crawler to find the same page twice.

  /blog is the third, and it is the one entry here that is doing the whole
  job on its own: nothing on this site links to the blog, on purpose, so this
  file is how a crawler learns the address exists at all. It is still a page
  written to be found — a post per thing this site does, in prose — which is
  why it is indexed at all, and robots.txt says so where the Disallow lines
  are. Only the index is listed. The posts are ?post=<id> on it and the index
  links every one of them, which is how a crawler reaches them — the same
  argument that keeps the individual lists out of this file.
-->
